{"kind":"report","text":"Architecture review of notes/2026-09-06-noseq-architecture.md at exact commit 2554c582db730cd3d64490970a573eab1463672e (artifact 0f3234d7), against request 87821661 and promise 2bea7548. The full review and a primary-source fact sheet are attached. No source changed and nothing is adopted.\n\nReadiness. The design is sound where it inherits gitseq and atseq: one order per instance, an opaque sequencer that runs no business fold, definitions pinned by content, deterministic bounded interpretation, projections as disposable caches. Those parts are ready to plan against. It is not yet ready for a detailed implementation plan, because five decisions it leaves open change the wire format, the trust model or the acceptance gates.\n\nBlocking findings (settle as written decisions before the plan):\nN1. No threat model, so \"confidential\" has no testable meaning; fix the first profile and make gate 7 assert exactly what the provider can see.\nN2. Forward secrecy and replayable history conflict. RFC 9750 and the adopted Marmot spec both say history recovery needs a separate application mechanism, and Marmot expires payloads past its window, so an archive is needed for every participant offline past it, not only late joiners. Choose a policy (long-lived wrapped content key is the smallest).\nN3. Signed wire format, proof retention and retry identity are unspecified. Sequence and predecessor must sit in signed tags or content; the entry must be a regular kind; stock relay limits and NIP-44 padding put the practical action ceiling near 24 KiB, so definitions and assets need blob storage from the start.\nN4. Membership authority is not located.\nN5. The Durable Object relay is a second protocol implementation with no bounded surface. Path-based endpoints are conformant; NIP-70 gives sequencer-only writes on a stock mirror; a stock strfry cannot restrict reads per instance, so the confidential-mirror question is real.\n\nBounded findings (fix inside the spike): N6 numeric and value profile for geometry; N7 direct UI projection needs the same capability boundary as definitions; N8 activation without a stale-submission rule; N9 invitation and device onboarding conflate three things, and immediate unattended admission is not available under Marmot at all; N10 equivocation detection has no mechanism; N11 Durable Object lifecycle corrections (transactional storage plus the output gate give durable-before-reply, awaits interleave, six outbound connections, 16 KiB socket attachment, 1000 rps soft limit, rows written dominate cost); N12 tests and the smallest deployment are described only as gates; N13 NIP-EE is superseded by Marmot (August 2026) and marmot-ts is alpha and predates that restructure, so both reuse claims are decisions in disguise.\n\nGates G1 to G10 that the plan must close are in the attached review. The fact sheet checks every NIP, RFC, Durable Object, strfry, Nosflare, Blossom and client-library claim against the current primary source: two outdated (NIP-EE, marmot-ts), one stricter than its source (relay endpoint paths), two useful omissions (NIP-70 protected events, NIP-11 limitation fields), and seven items that could not be confirmed.","body":{"blocking":"N1,N2,N3,N4,N5","head":"2554c582db730cd3d64490970a573eab1463672e","readiness":"not-ready-for-detailed-plan"}}